INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_full_text` VALUES
(1026939,73272,'HJ14',30,'2024-01-04','HOUSE JOINT RESOLUTION NO. 14\r </b></center>\r <center>\r Offered January 10, 2024\r </center>\r <center>\r Prefiled January 4, 2024\r </center>\r <i>Extending state recognition to the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe \r  of Virginia.</i>\r <center>----------</center>\r <center>\r Patrons-- McQuinn, Cole, Hope, LeVere Bolling, Martinez, Simon and Simonds\r </center>\r <center>----------</center>\r <center>\r Committee Referral Pending\r </center>\r <center>----------</center>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee \r  Tribe of Virginia are a Native American people who are ancestors of the \r  original Cherokee of Virginia and now linguistically a branch of the Iroquoian \r  language group; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, Dr. Walter Ashby Plecker, serving as the first \r  registrar for the newly created Virginia Bureau of Vital Statistics from 1912 \r  to 1946, denied Cherokee Indians and other Indian tribes the ability to verify \r  their continuous heritage through the use of accurate and legally binding \r  documents; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, several tribal members of the Wolf Creek Cherokee \r  Tribe have been verified as meeting the definition of Native American by the \r  Commonwealth of Virginia by being recognized as Native American minority \r  business owners; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe, \r  Inc., of Virginia, a nonprofit organization \r  granted § 501(c)(3) \r  status by the Internal Revenue Service, is dedicated to maintaining the \r  Cherokee culture and the heritage of the tribe \r  through ongoing education, preservation, and community outreach, including participation in cultural events that educate \r  and support goodwill such as powwows, school \r  programs, cultural and musical performances, and performances at military \r  institutions; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, members of the Wolf Creek \r  Cherokee Tribe represent the Cherokee people who have lived and continue to \r  live in the Commonwealth of Virginia and they celebrate diversity, equity, and \r  inclusion of all citizens of the Commonwealth; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, members of the Wolf Creek \r  Cherokee Tribe are all blood kinship, related to \r  one another with ancestral heritage in the Commonwealth and recognized by the \r  general public; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, members of the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe are from the \r  areas of Price Mountain, Milam Ridge, and Wolf Creek Mountain; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the history of Cherokee families is well-documented in the Commonwealth, \r  and Virginia promotes tourism connected with the tribe; \r  the Wolf Creek Cherokee Museum and Tribal Center is featured on the Virginia is for Lovers website and also \r  listed on visitrichmond.com and the Henrico County tourism website; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Museum \r  and Tribal Center is located at 7400 Osborne Turnpike in Henrico County and is \r  open free of charge to the public to view presentations of \r  tribal history and the archaeology of its areas; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, through its museum and tribal center, the Wolf \r  Creek Cherokee Tribe preserves Native American \r  artifacts, displays artwork, \r  provides opportunities for genealogical research, \r  and sponsors educational classes; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe, Inc., of Virginia received the Henrico County Historic Preservation Award \r  2017 for maintaining the history of its tribe; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe, Inc., of Virginia \r  represents itself through cultural and historical \r  performances at festivals in Henrico and Chesterfield \r  Counties and in various cities and other counties of \r  the Commonwealth; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, on November 24, 2015, members of the Wolf Creek \r  Cherokee Tribe performed during the Fort Lee Native \r  American Observance Ceremony and were commended by \r  Brigadier General Ronald Kirklin of the U.S. \r  Army; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, on November 1, 2017, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe \r  performed a cultural and historical program for the Virginia Commonwealth \r  University Office of Multicultural Student Affairs, presenting its Cherokee Virginia history for Native American \r  Heritage Month; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe is recognized by the \r  military bases and school systems of the Commonwealth, as it is called upon to \r  provide Cherokee cultural and historical presentations; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe has been featured in \r  several programs and articles in a variety of publications with circulation in the Commonwealth, including (i) \"An Identity Denied,\" by Joe Heim, <i>The Washington \r  Post</i>, July 2, 2015; (ii) \"Museum \r  Tells Story of Wolf Creek Cherokees,\" by Bill Lohman, <i>Richmond \r  Times-Dispatch</i>, July 5, 2015; (iii) \"Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe Moves Museum to \r  Varina,\" by Eileen Mellon, <i>Henrico \r  Citizen Magazine</i>, July 2, 2015; (iv) \r  \"Heritage on Display,\" by Bill Lohman, <i>Richmond Times-Dispatch</i>, July 26, 2015; (v) <i>Virginia Currents Magazine</i>, Catherine Komp, \r  September 15, 2015; (vi) \"Wolf Creek Cherokee \r  Tribe Drum Circle,\" by Justin Vaughan and Maureen McNabb, <i>Richmond \r  Magazine</i>, online, September 16, 2015; (vii) \r  \"Native Identity,\" by Tharon Giddens, <i>Richmond \r  Magazine</i>, October 2015; (viii) \"Exposing \r  \'layers of history\' in Henrico,\" by Laura Kebede, <i>Richmond Times-Dispatch</i>, November 19, 2015; \r  (ix) \"Digging up the past in Henrico,\" by Bob Brown, containing \r  interviews with Chief Terry Price and Virginia Archaeologist Harry Jaeger, <i>Richmond Times-Dispatch</i>, online, \r  November 19, 2015; (x) programs \r  on HCTV Channel 17, featuring a Varina High School student interview with Wolf Creek \r  Cherokee Tribe members and a Wolf Creek Cherokee \r  Tribe Native American program at Holman Middle School; and (xi) \"Hidden History, Henrico County \r  Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe drums to keep history alive,\" by Jonathan \r  Costen, April 5, 2017, 8 News, WRIC; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, in May 2017, Chief Terry Price of the Wolf Creek \r  Cherokee Tribe, Inc., of Virginia initiated a Native American cultural outreach \r  program to Virginia\'s short-term inmates that is recognized by the Virginia Department of \r  Corrections; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, in October 2017, Chief Terry Price and tribal members \r  were recognized as they gave a lecture to counselors at Henrico County Mental \r  Health Services during Suicide Prevention Month to promote well-being for \r  Henrico citizens; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee \r  Tribe\'s ancestors made contact with the Spanish explorers \r  in the areas of Southwest Virginia near Saltville 80 years before \r  English explorers and therefore were later called Melungeons due to the \r  influence by the Spanish/Portuguese; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the article, \"Surviving Indian Groups of the \r  Eastern United States,\" by William Harlen Gilbert, \r  Jr., published in the 1948 annual report of the Board of Regents of the \r  Smithsonian Institution lists the Ramps and \r  Melungeons Tribe of Virginia with more than 3,000 \r  members who have been defined as the Wolf Creek \r  Cherokee Tribe\'s ancestors; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Tribe, Inc., of Virginia offers the \r  following references in verifying that the Ramps or Melungeons (same and \r  interchangeable) of Virginia are the Cherokee of Virginia:</p>\r  \r   <p>1. \"Surviving Indian Groups of the Eastern United \r  States,\" William Harlen Gilbert, Jr., Annual \r  Report of the Board of Regents of the Smithsonian Institution, 1948.</p>\r  \r   <p>2. \"Surviving Indian Groups of the Eastern United \r  States,\" William A. Brophy and Sophie Aberle \r  Brophy Papers, 1923–1973, Commissioner of Indian Affairs, 1945–1948, Box 94, \r  Harry S. Truman Library and Museum. Ramps and Melungeons Tribe of Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>3. Letter to the Secretary of State, Nashville, Tennessee, \r  from Walter Plecker, August 5, 1942, regarding Melungeons in Southwest \r  Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>4. <i>The Melungeons</i>, Bonnie Ball, Historical Society of \r  Southwest Virginia, 1966. The Melungeons were called \r  Ramps by their neighbors.</p>\r  \r   <p>5. Letter of Hamilton McMillan to Indian Office, July 17, \r  1890, Indians of North Carolina: A Letter from the Secretary of the Interior, \r  Exhibit B7.</p>\r  \r   <p>6. Smithsonian Institution, Bureau of American Ethnology, \r  1907, p. 365. Melungeons found in North Carolina and Tennessee, all of these \r  are local designations for people of mixed race with an Indian nucleus, differing in no way from the present mixed blood \r  remnants known as Pamunkey, Chickahominy, and \r  Nansemond Indians in Virginia, except in the more \r  complete loss of their identity.</p>\r  \r   <p>7. Annual Report of the Board of Regents of the Smithsonian \r  Institution, No. 24, December 10, 1948. Tennessee - the Cherokee are very few \r  and located in the eastern mountain counties. Also in the Appalachian great \r  valley area.</p>\r  \r   <p>8. \"Genealogical History of the Melungeons \r  Families,\" Mark French Jr., History from George \r  Washington Osborne of Copper Ridge near Dungannon in Scott County, Virginia. \r  George Washington Osborne is an ancestor of the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe.</p>\r  \r   <p>9. \"A Strange People,\" Will Allen Dromgoole, <i>Nashville \r  Sunday American</i>, September 1, 1890. Reference to \r  full-blooded Indian, Cherokee chief, and Melungeon customs.</p>\r  \r   <p>10. \"The Melungens,\" \r  unknown journalist, <i>Littell\'s Living Age</i>, March 1849. Reference to the \r  Melungeons of Powell\'s Mountain and Copper Ridge Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>11. \"Peculiar Peoples in \r  America,\" Frederic J. Haskins, <i>Dallas \r  Morning News</i>, June 23, 1907. Malungeon resemble the Cherokee and were part \r  of the Cherokees who refused to go West.</p>\r  \r   <p>12. Interview of Lewis Jarvis published in the Hancock County \r  Times, 1903. Reference to Melungeons coming from Cumberland County and New \r  River Virginia. Mentions Stoney Creek, Scott County, Virginia, Fort Blackmore. \r  Also states: \"people were quite full blooded.\"</p>\r  \r   <p>13. Letter of Mrs. John Trotwood Moore dated August 12, 1942, \r  in response to a letter from Walter Plecker. These people are friendly to the \r  Cherokee and came west from Cumberland County, Virginia. Most Melungeons went \r  to the Union Army. References to Stony Creek, Scott County, and Fort Blackmore.</p>\r  \r   <p>14. \"Battle of Sappony Church, Sussex County, Virginia, \r  June 28, 1864,\" <i>Daily South Carolinian</i>, July 1864. \"Were these \r  Virginia Molungeons different from the Tennesse Melungeons? Or were they \r  kin?\"</p>\r  \r   <p>15. \"The Remnant of an Indian Race,\" John B. \r  Brownlow, <i>Watson\'s Jeffersonian Magazine</i>, 1911, p. 522. A mixture of \r  these Portuguese with the Cherokee Indians, but not with Negros.</p>\r  \r   <p>16. <i>Atlanta Constitution</i>, March 15, 1891 (reporting \r  on). \"Will Allen Dromgoole in the March Arena \r  gives account of the Malungeons, \'in appearance they bear a \r  striking resemblance to the Cherokees, and they are \r  believed by the people round about to be a kind of half breed Indian.\'\"</p>\r  \r   <p>17. Report on Indians Taxed and Indians Not Taxed in the \r  United States (Except Alaska) at the Eleventh Census: 1890, Washington D.C., \r  U.S. Census Printing Office, p. 594. Popularly known as Melungeons in addition \r  to those still known as Cherokee. Trace descent to two Cherokee Indians.</p>\r  \r   <p>18. Quotes of persons of Scott County who have heard their \r  families called Ramps.</p>\r  \r   <p>19. \"Memorandum Concerning \r  the Characteristics of the Larger Mixed-Blood Racial Islands of the Eastern \r  United States,\" William Harlen Gilbert, Jr., <i>Social Forces</i>, Vol. 24, No. 4 (May 1946), \r  pp. 438–477. In Southwest Virginia, they are also known as Ramps and can occur \r  in the counties of Giles, Lee, Russell, Scott, Washington, and Wise. List of \r  surnames include those of the members of the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe, Inc., \r  of Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>20. \"Memorandum Concerning the Characteristics of the \r  Larger Mixed-Blood Racial Islands of the Eastern United States,\" William \r  Harlen Gilbert, Jr., <i>Social Forces</i>, Vol. 24, \r  No. 4 (May 1946), pp. 438–477. Melungeons of the Southern Appalachians. Relief: \r  Were given food and clothing in Virginia during the Depression of the 1930s.</p>\r  \r   <p>21. \"New Native American Haplogroup,\" Roberta Estes, \r  December 19, 2010. DNA Explanations, Native American.</p>\r  \r   <p>22. \"Melungeons, A Multi-Ethnic Population,\" Roberta \r  J. Estes, Jack H. Goins, Penny Ferguson, Janet Lewis Crain, <i>Journal of \r  Genetic Genealogy</i>, Vol. 7. (Fall 2011). Sizemore ancestors of the Wolf \r  Creek Cherokee Tribe, Inc., of Virginia; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the following 31 enumerated references are offered by \r  the Wolf Creek Tribe, Inc., of Virginia as proof of the Cherokee history in \r  Virginia:</p>\r  \r   <p>1. \"Four Thousand Years of \r  Native American Cave Art in the Southern Appalachians,\" \r  Charles H. Faulkner, Department of Anthropology, University of Tennessee, <i>Journal \r  of Cave and Karst Studies</i>, December 1997.</p>\r  \r   <p>2. Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia. The first Anglo-Cherokee \r  contact was made in 1654. English settlers fought the Powhatan confederacy and \r  600 Cherokee settled in abandoned Powhatan lands in Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>3. Cherokee Nation of Oklahoma; Tribal website.</p>\r  \r   <p>4. Eastern Band of Cherokee website.</p>\r  \r   <p>5. \"Indians Struggle for \r  Recognition in their Own Land,\" RVA News, May 26, 2015. Capital News \r  Service states Virginia Indians were victims of what some call paper genocide.</p>\r  \r   <p>6. Wikipedia, free encyclopedia. Native tribes in Virginia, \r  includes Cherokee of Southwest Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>7. \"Osteological Comparison of Prehistoric Native \r  American from Southwest Virginia and East Tennessee Mortuary Caves,\" C. \r  Clifford Boyd, Jr., and \r  Donna C. Boyd, Department of Sociology and Anthropology, Radford University, \r  Radford, Virginia, <i>Journal of Cave and Karst Studies</i>, December 1997.</p>\r  \r   <p>8. Federal Register, Vol. 74, No. \r  87, Thursday, May 7, 2009, Department of the \r  Interior, National Park Service Notice of Inventory Completion; Virginia \r  Department of Conservation and Recreation, Division of State Parks, Richmond, \r  Virginia, and Southwest Virginia Museum Historical State Park, Big Stone Gap, \r  Virginia Agency; National Park Service Interior; Notice: Study of Cherokee \r  Territorial Control of this Area of Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>9. Virginia Department of Education study guide; map of \r  Cherokee occupation of Virginia, description of Cherokee people of Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>10. \"The Pocahontas Exception: The Exemption of American Indian \r  Ancestry from Racial Purity Law,\" Kevin Noble Maillard, <i>Michigan \r  Journal of Race and Law</i>, Spring 2007. Families with ancestry of the \r  Powhatan Tribe only were exempt from the Racial Integrity Act.</p>\r  \r   <p>11. \"A Sketch of the Early History of Southwestern Virginia,\" \r  Ralph M. Brown, <i>William and Mary Quarterly</i> 2nd Ser., Vol. 17, No. 4, \r  Oct. 1937. The Cherokee Apparenny permitted the remnants of the Siouans to live \r  undisturbed in the Southwest of Virginia. The Siouans being the Monacans.</p>\r  \r   <p>12. <i>The Virginia Indian Heritage Trail</i>, edited by Karenne Wood, The Virginia Foundation for the Humanities, \r  2009, 3rd ed. page 14. Native languages and corresponding Virginia tribes: \r  Iroquoian, Cherokee, Nottaway, and Meherrin.</p>\r  \r   <p>13. Recognition of Indian Tribes, Secretary of Natural \r  Resources, Report Document No. 74 (2014), Virginia Legislative Information \r  System. The General Assembly was confronted with several resolutions from \r  groups with possible association with the historical Cherokee habitation of \r  Southwest Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>14. Smithsonian Institution map, 1974. Showing Cherokee \r  occupation in Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>15. <i>Virginia History</i>, Keith Egloff, assistant curator, \r  Virginia Department of Historic Resources, Richmond, Virginia and William B. Obrochta, \r  assistant director of education, Virginia Historical Society, Richmond, \r  Virginia. (A Virginia history textbook used in the \r  public school system in 2015 that states the \r  historic occupation of Cherokee in Virginia and includes a map.)</p>\r  \r   <p>16. Virginia is for Lovers. Promotion of the history of \r  Cherokee people in the Southwest of Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>17. \"Indians of Virginia – The Real First Families of \r  Virginia,\" <i>Virginia Places</i>, October 2015. Explorers identified \r  tribes such as the Cherokee.</p>\r  \r   <p>18. \"Amherst County Indians,\" Edgar Whitehouse, <i>Richmond \r  Times Dispatch</i>, April 19, 1896. Virginia Indian Archive called it a highly interesting history of an old settlement of Cherokees.</p>\r  \r   <p>19. <i>Virginia Indian Archive</i>, January 29, 2005. Quote of \r  Chief Wayne Adkins: \"It\'s hard to go anyplace without somebody saying I \r  have a Cherokee grandmother.\"</p>\r  \r   <p>20. Virginia is for Lovers, Big Stone Gap. The story begins \r  with the Cherokee.</p>\r  \r   <p>21. \"The Problem of the Rechahecrian Indians of \r  Virginia,\" William Wallace Tooker, <i>The American Anthropological \r  Association</i>, Vol. 11, No. 9, September 1898. The Rechahecrian Indians who \r  fought at the Battle of Bloody Run near Richmond were, \r  in fact, Cherokee.</p>\r  \r   <p>22. National Register of Historic Places, Fort Chiswell, Wythe \r  County, Virginia. Settlers were not to pass Fort Chiswell as the land beyond \r  belonged to the Cherokee.</p>\r  \r   <p>23. National Register of Historic \r  Places, Gala Site, Botetourt County, Virginia. Complex history of influences \r  including Mississippian Cherokee cultures.</p>\r  \r   <p>24. New River Notes, Flower Swift Company, Montgomery County, \r  Virginia, 1779–1783. A reference to the Osborne family descendants of the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe, Osborne Cemetery.</p>\r  \r   <p>25. \"An Archeological Survey of Southwest Virginia,\" \r  C. G. Holland, <i>Smithsonian Contribution to Anthropology</i>, Number 12, \r  1970. Report on Cherokee artifacts unearthed in Southwest Virginia categorized \r  and sent to the Smithsonian Institution.</p>\r  \r   <p>26. National Register of Historic Places, St. Paul Historic \r  District, Wise County, Virginia. The colonial government had good relations \r  with the Cherokees and they were acknowledged as having rights to most of \r  Southwest Virginia in treaty negotiations.</p>\r  \r   <p>27. \"The Lost World of Rocketts Landing,\" Matt \r  Gottlieb, <i>Notes on Virginia, Virginia Department of Historic Resources</i>, \r  Number 49, 2005. At Rocketts, whites, free blacks, \r  and mulattoes along with slaves and European immigrants and Cherokee and other \r  Virginia Indians labor together.</p>\r  \r   <p>28. <i>American Anthropologist</i>, Vol. 11, No. 9, Sept. \r  1898, pp. <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/261-270\">261-270</a>. The tradition as to those who came from Virginia seeks also \r  to establish the idea that the Powhatan Indians were Cherokees.</p>\r  \r   <p>29. <i>Virginia\'s First People Past &amp; Present</i>, \r  Virginia Department of Education (present geography maps and regions). Others \r  recognized Cherokee claim to Southwestern Virginia.</p>\r  \r   <p>30. Memoirs of Lt. Henry Timberlake who lived with the \r  Cherokee of Virginia, 1765.</p>\r  \r   <p>31. State Corporation Commission, Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe, \r  Inc., Class states Cherokee American Indians; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, precedent has been set by the states of Georgia and \r  Alabama, who have recognized Native American \r  Cherokee tribes at the state level; and</p>\r  \r   <p>WHEREAS, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe of Virginia has \r  submitted all filings and complied with all requests for information from the \r  Secretary of the Commonwealth and the Virginia Indian Advisory Board; now, \r  therefore, be it</p>\r  \r   <p>RESOLVED by the House of Delegates, the Senate concurring, \r  That from and after the effective date of this resolution, the General Assembly \r  of Virginia extend state recognition to the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe of \r  Virginia; and, be it</p>\r  \r   <p>RESOLVED FURTHER, That the Clerk of the \r  House of Delegates transmit a copy of this resolution to Chief Terry \r  Price of the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe, Inc., of \r  Virginia, requesting that he further disseminate copies of this resolution to \r  his constituents so that they may be apprised of the sense of the General \r  Assembly of Virginia in this matter; and, be it</p>\r  \r   <p>RESOLVED FURTHER, That the General Assembly of Virginia, by \r  this resolution, does not address the question of \r  whether the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe of Virginia has been continuously in \r  existence since the 1600s; and, be it</p>\r  \r   <p>RESOLVED FURTHER, That the General Assembly of Virginia, by \r  this resolution, does not confirm, confer, grant, or recognize any rights or \r  privileges, including any vested or nonvested rights to property real or \r  personal, to the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe of Virginia under any law, treaty, \r  or other agreements; and, be it</p>\r  \r   <p>RESOLVED FINALLY, That the General Assembly of Virginia, by \r  this resolution, does not confirm, confer, or address in \r  any manner any issues of sovereignty.</p>',NULL,0,'2024-01-05 15:55:03','2025-11-06 00:15:32');
