INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(21,77039,31,'The bill, which proposed allowing by-right multifamily development in areas zoned for commercial use, was introduced in the Senate and referred to the Committee on Local Government. It failed to advance after being defeated in the committee with a narrow vote of 5 in favor, 7 against, and 3 abstentions. As a result, the bill did not progress further in the legislative process.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:33','2026-01-09 04:10:33');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(22,76873,31,'The bill regarding student cell phone and smart device possession in public schools was introduced and passed in both the Senate and the House, though it faced narrow votes in each chamber. After initial disagreements on amendments, a conference committee resolved the differences, and both chambers agreed to the conference report. The governor recommended further changes, which were accepted unanimously by both chambers, and the bill was enacted into law, effective July 1, 2025.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:35','2026-01-09 04:10:35');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(23,77034,31,'The bill proposing a tax credit for veterinary care for retired police canines was introduced and referred to the Committee on Finance. It was assigned to a subcommittee and placed on the Finance agenda but did not advance further. Ultimately, the bill was left in the Finance Committee and did not proceed to a vote, effectively halting its progress.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:38','2026-01-09 04:10:38');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(24,77430,31,'The bill, concerning nutritional standards for school meals, was introduced in the House and passed unanimously with amendments. It then moved to the Senate, where it also passed unanimously with further amendments. The House agreed to the Senate\'s amendments, and the bill was signed by both the Speaker and the President before being approved by the Governor, becoming law effective July 1, 2027.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:41','2026-01-09 04:10:41');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(25,77318,31,'The bill regarding state correctional facilities\' conditions and temperature monitoring passed both the House and Senate, though with relatively narrow margins. It was enrolled and sent to the Governor, who subsequently vetoed it. The House then sustained the Governor\'s veto, preventing the bill from becoming law.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:43','2026-01-09 04:10:43');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(26,77399,31,'The bill, which aimed to prohibit interbasin transfers of water, was introduced and referred to the Committee on Agriculture, Conservation and Natural Resources, where it was unanimously reported with a substitute and then sent to the Finance and Appropriations Committee. However, it was left in the Finance and Appropriations Committee without a vote, effectively halting its progress. As a result, the bill did not advance to become law.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:45','2026-01-09 04:10:45');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(27,76972,31,'The bill was introduced and referred to the Committee on Commerce and Labor. It was later withdrawn by the sponsor at their request, with a unanimous committee vote agreeing to strike it. As a result, the bill did not proceed further in the legislative process.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:46','2026-01-09 04:10:46');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(28,76995,31,'The bill to update child support amounts passed the Senate with a 32-7 vote and the House with a 77-19 vote. After being enrolled and signed by the legislative leaders, it was sent to the Governor, who made a recommendation that the Senate later rejected. The bill was then communicated back to the Governor for further action.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:48','2026-01-09 04:10:48');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(29,76483,31,'The bill aimed at enhancing civics education was introduced by a Republican and referred to the House Committee on Education. The Education subcommittee recommended continuing the bill to 2025, and this decision was confirmed by a voice vote in the full committee. Ultimately, the bill was left in the Education Committee and did not advance further in the legislative process.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:51','2026-01-09 04:10:51');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(30,78711,31,'The bill requiring food allergy awareness notices in restaurants was introduced and passed by both the Senate and the House, with amendments, receiving a mix of unanimous and near-unanimous support in the Senate and a narrower margin in the House. After being enrolled and signed by the legislative leaders, it was sent to the governor, who vetoed it. The Senate then sustained the governor\'s veto, preventing the bill from becoming law.','y','2026-01-09 04:10:53','2026-01-09 04:10:53');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(31,78827,31,'The bill, which sought to impose restrictions on restorative housing and isolated confinement, was introduced by a Democrat and passed through the Senate with a narrow vote of 21-18. It then passed the House with a similarly close vote of 50-47. However, the bill was ultimately vetoed by the Republican governor, and the Senate sustained the veto, preventing it from becoming law.','y','2026-01-09 11:01:47','2026-01-09 11:01:47');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(32,78195,31,'The bill regarding compulsory attendance requirements for public schools, with a religious exemption, was introduced and initially moved forward by the Education and Health Committee with a substitute. However, it was later sent to the Finance and Appropriations Committee, where it was effectively halted by a vote to pass it by indefinitely, with a significant majority voting in favor of this action. As a result, the bill did not progress further and was not enacted into law.','y','2026-01-09 11:01:49','2026-01-09 11:01:49');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(34,77600,31,'The bill, which proposed a tax exemption for the purchase of motor vehicle child restraint devices, was introduced and referred to the Committee on Finance. It was then assigned to a Finance subcommittee and later referred to the Committee on Appropriations. However, the bill did not advance further as it was left in the Appropriations Committee, effectively halting its progress.','y','2026-01-09 11:01:54','2026-01-09 11:01:54');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(38,76924,31,'The bill concerning the classification of land and improvements for tax purposes was introduced by a Republican and referred to the Committee on Finance. It was further assigned to a Finance subcommittee but did not advance beyond this point. Ultimately, the bill was left in the Finance Committee and did not proceed to a vote, effectively halting its progress.','y','2026-01-09 11:02:04','2026-01-09 11:02:04');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(39,78905,31,'The bill on expungement and sealing of criminal records passed the House with a strong majority and the Senate with a narrower vote. After the Senate\'s substitute was rejected by the House, a conference committee resolved the differences, and both chambers agreed to the conference report. The governor recommended changes, which were unanimously accepted by both the House and Senate, and the bill was enacted into law, effective July 1, 2025.','y','2026-01-09 11:02:07','2026-01-09 11:02:07');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(36,76777,31,'The bill aimed at allowing local prohibition or regulation of gas-powered leaf blowers was introduced and referred to the Senate Committee on Local Government. It was discussed with amendments and substitutes offered but was ultimately continued to 2025 by a vote of 11 in favor and 4 against. The bill was later left in the committee, effectively halting its progress.','y','2026-01-09 11:02:00','2026-01-09 11:02:00');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `bills_status_narratives` VALUES
(1317,77635,31,'The bill, introduced by a Republican, was referred to the House Committee on Education and assigned to the K-12 Subcommittee. Despite being placed on the Education agenda, it was left in the committee and did not advance further. As a result, the bill did not pass into law.','y','2026-02-03 15:02:06','2026-02-03 15:02:06');
