(16089,90233,72980,'Rudie Verougstraete','example@example.com',NULL,'127.68.177.142','Because we all know emergencies never happen at schools. Especially since we never do anything to stop mass shootings.','comment','published','n','2024-01-19 23:14:29','2024-01-19 23:14:29');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16254,91012,76995,'Hans','example@example.com',NULL,'127.173.178.172','Please vote against SB 805, which would carry Virginia even further away from compliance with federal child-support regulations, by imposing excessive child support obligations on thousands of parents that they will be unable to pay, and leave them unable to meet their basic needs. SB 805\'s schedule of basic child support obligations violates 45 CFR § 302.56(c)(1).\n\n45 CFR § 302.56(c)(1) requires state child-support guidelines to take into account the \"ability to pay\" and \"the basic subsistence needs of the noncustodial parent.\"\n\nTo comply with that regulation, adopted in 2016, Virginia\'s neighbors, such as North Carolina, Maryland, and Tennessee, do not impose significant child support obligations on parents making less than $1300 per month, because parents that poor cannot afford to pay much child support while housing and feeding themselves.  (In Maryland, the basic child support amount is $50-$60 per month for parents making $1200 or less per month, and $109-$124 per month for parents making $1300 per month. See Md. Code 12-204.In North Carolina, the basic child support obligation is only $50 per month for parents making $1300 per month. See North Carolina Schedule of Basic Support Obligations, effective January 1, 2023). In Tennessee, the child-support \"guidelines include a SSR [self-support reserve] that ensures obligors have sufficient income to maintain a minimum standard of living based on 90% of the 2020 federal poverty level for one person ($957 gross income per month).\" See page 11, Rules of the Tennessee Department of Human Services, Child Support Services Division, Chapter 1240-02-04, Child Support Guideline.)\n\nBut under this child-support bill (SB 805), the basic child support obligation is an unaffordable $264 to $665 per month for households making $1300 per month (depending on the number of kids they have). For parents making $550 per month, basic child support is between $114 and $287 per month, which is well beyond their ability to pay. \n\nSo under the bill, even parents way below the poverty level have to pay 20% to 52% of their income in basic child support -- leaving them with too little money to pay rent! Even the poorest parents unable to pay child support are ordered to pay a big fraction of their income in child support under the bill.\n\nSB 805 not only continues to impose child support obligations on parents making as little as $350 per month (most of whom simply cannot pay such obligations), it increases child support obligations for households with incomes as little as $550 per month. At some higher income ranges, the increases in child support are over 30%, and child support obligors have to pay tens of thousands of dollars per year in child support.\n \nIt is impractical for the bill to increase child support obligations for parents making very low incomes such as $550 per month or even $2,000 per month, which is still below the income made by many minimum wage workers in Virginia (the state hourly minimum wage is $12.41).\n\nAnd this is just part of the child-support many parents owe. SB 805 increases the single most important component of child-support obligations, the schedule of basic child support obligations contained in Va. Code § 20-108.2(B)..\n\nBut that is just the beginning of what many parents owe in child support. Additional amounts of child support are also imposed on noncustodial parents under other existing provisions of Virginia law, such as for \"health care coverage\", see Va. Code § 20-108.2(E), and for \"child care\" costs, see Va. Code § 20-108.2(F), which are awarded on top of the schedule of basic child support obligations. For low-income parents, those amounts can be bigger than the amount of basic child support, resulting in an overall child-support obligation that consumes most of their income (even before taxes). For example, a father was ordered to pay $673 in monthly child support on his monthly income of just $1300, back when Virginia\'s child support obligations were lower than they are today, see the Virginia Court of Appeals decision in Herring v. Herring, 532 S.E.2d 923, 925 (2000).\n\nSo even if parents could pay the amounts contained in SB 805\'s schedule of basic child support obligations (many parents could not), they still might not be able to pay their overall child support obligation.Even the portions of the child support schedule (in SB 805) that look reasonable on first glance may not be, because they are just part of the child support obligations imposed on many parents, which include things like additional child care expenses.\n\nI am not a noncustodial parent, but I and my wife have been parents for nearly 18 years, and based on my experience as a parent, it costs less to raise a kid than the Virginia child-support guidelines typically award in child support for middle class parents like us. Thus, the guidelines are more than adequate and do not need to be increased further.\n\nExcessive child support levels can backfire by reducing the earning capacity of noncustodial parents and leaving them even less able to pay taxes and child support in the future. In 2019, The Baltimore Sun quoted a former federal head of child support enforcement, Vicki Turetsky, noting that \"Child support orders set beyond the ability of noncustodial parents to comply push them out of low-wage jobs, drown them in debt, hound them into the underground economy, and chase them out of their children’s lives.\"\n\nAs the Baltimore Sun noted, when noncustodial parents \"fall behind on payments, they can lose their driver’s licenses and professional licenses...The enforcement tactics can make it hard for them to catch back up on payments...Evidence shows fathers who get into deep debt are less engaged with their kids, contributing to greater rates of depression, alcohol use, poor health and progressively worse behavior by the children.\"\n\nChild support obligations are often set too high to begin with. When California commissioned the Urban Institute to investigate why parents were often behind on their child support, it reported that the number one reason for arrearages was that “orders are set too high relative to ability to pay.”\n\nI was the intake attorney for a nonprofit law firm for 6 years. We didn\'t handle child-support or family law cases, but despite that, I received innumerable letters and emails from people ordered to pay more than they could possibly afford in child support. It was obvious they were drowning in debt, and unlikely to ever pay what they owed in full. SB805 would make that problem worse.','comment','published','n','2025-01-04 01:39:03','2025-01-04 01:39:03'),
(16255,91012,76995,'Hans','example@example.com',NULL,'127.173.206.195','Existing child-support obligations are already excessive for large numbers of low-income noncustodial parents, due to the child-support guidelines\' failure to take into account the public benefits that go to custodial parents and most of the tax benefits that go to custodial parents. Child support obligations are often excessive because Virginia\'s child support guidelines don\'t take into account tax benefits and other public benefits custodial parents get on account of their kids (such as the $2,000 per child tax credit, subsidized housing, TANF, etc.), in determining the amount of child support that the custodial parent needs and the non-custodial parent owes. That results in the non-custodial parent paying household expenses of the custodial parent that are already paid for by the tax code and other sources -- expenses low-income noncustodial parents can\'t afford to pay while also paying for their own housing, food & clothing. For example, Virginia Code Section 20-108.2(C) says, \"\'Gross income\' shall not include: 1. Benefits from public assistance and social services programs as defined in § 63.2-100; 2. Federal supplemental security income benefits...\" Virginia law also defines as income to the noncustodial parent things that aren\'t income under the federal tax code.... \"custodial parents will get the child tax credits (and other credits).\" But these tax credits aren\'t treated as income to the custodial parent in calculating the child support needed to be paid to the custodial parent, even though they are effectively income to the custodial parent.\"\n\nThe $2,000 child tax credit, earned-income tax credits, and other public benefits don\'t reduce the noncustodial parent\'s child-support obligation. In Virginia, unlike most states, judges apparently have no authority to order the custodial parent to waive the child tax exemption or credit so that it may be claimed by the non-custodial parent, even if the non-custodial parent provides most of the financial support for the child. See Floyd v. Floyd, 436 S.E.2d 451, 463 (Va. 1997); Pearlene Anklesaria, Child-Related Tax Breaks for Divorced Parents, 22 J. Am. Acad. of Matrim. L. 425, 426-27 (2009) (contrasting Virginia law with the law of most states). The custodial parent is also more able than the non-custodial parent to claim the Earned Income Tax Credit. Those tax credits and other public benefits don\'t reduce the non-custodial parent\'s child support obligation. See, e.g., Va. Code § 20-108.2(C) (defining \"gross income\" to exclude \"Benefits from public assistance and social services programs\").\n\nSo SB 805 would make matters worse by increasing already-excessive child-support obligations of many low-income parents.','comment','published','n','2025-01-05 16:48:30','2025-01-05 16:48:30');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16012,90246,73272,'Opteniak','example@example.com',NULL,'127.181.56.114','They have already been rejected by the Commonwealth and found to be at best a \"family\" not a \"tribe\". I guess they don\'t want to say look we aren\'t a tribe but please make us one. This bill should be killed again. It has already been killed several times. They are not an Indian Tribe even though they claim to be. At best and in all probability are what one would call \"Pretendians\"','comment','published','n','2024-01-13 15:09:11','2024-01-13 15:09:11'),
(16121,90332,73272,'Annette','example@example.com',NULL,'127.110.194.93','In this time of equity and inclusion of all people, it so good to finally see we will include the Cherokee peoples of Virginia. The Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe of Virginia are so well known for their work providing cultural, history and education to Virginia through their tribal center and Museum as well as their programs in archaeology.  Everyone should have the right to stand as who they are, not have a committee make decisions on race. This is probably the only tribe of Virginia who carry birth records stating “Indian”.  I see that there are obviously people who have fear of recognition of this tribe.  The Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe of Virginia will continue their work. They will continue to exist and will become the 12 th state recognized tribe in Virginia. The Virginia Indian Advisory Board and their findings were bias and influenced by outside persons, such as the Cherokee Nation of Oklahoma.  Persons who do note vote or pay taxes in Virginia.','comment','published','n','2024-01-22 13:57:05','2024-01-22 13:57:05'),
(16150,90416,73272,'GenocideM','example@example.com',NULL,'127.119.44.98','In the relentless pursuit of recognition, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe\'s narrative emerges not only as a testament to fortitude but as a forceful formal accusation against a system riddled with bureaucratic hurdles, deliberate obstructions, due process violations, targeted malice, and civil rights transgressions. Their journey to secure state acknowledgment thrusts them into a legal battleground where the complexities of their struggle transcend mere bureaucratic challenges.\n\nThis legal odyssey lays bare a profound tale of systematic obstructionism, where deliberate barriers have been strategically erected to impede the tribe\'s path to recognition. The narrative resonates with the echoes of due process violations, wherein constitutional safeguards have been callously trampled upon, leaving the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe exposed to the malevolent whims of biased entities.\n\nThe targeted malice becomes a stark reality as legislative maneuvers, epitomized by the imposition of federal regulations, unfold as a calculated assault on the tribe\'s rightful claim to recognition. These actions transcend administrative missteps, revealing a corrosive erosion of justice that strikes at the very core of constitutional principles.\n\nCivil rights violations further darken this narrative, as the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe finds itself subjected to public humiliation, orchestrated defamation, and a systematic attack on their heritage. The Advisory Board\'s actions transform the pursuit of recognition into a diplomatic battleground, where the tribe, undeterred, demands understanding while facing the chasm in knowledge and perception.\n\nYet, this is not merely a tale of victimhood; it is a resolute pursuit of justice. The Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe\'s legal journey unfolds as a powerful counterforce against historical wrongs. As they navigate this intricate terrain, the concluding notes resonate not with a plea but with a fervent demand for accountability. \n\nThe path forward is not merely suggested; it is illuminated with the possibilities of legal avenues to challenge impositions, unveil biased proceedings, and hold those accountable who perpetuated the systemic injustice.\n\nIn the corridors of justice, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe\'s resolute footsteps leave an indelible mark. Their pursuit is not just a legal endeavor; it is a bold confrontation with systemic oppression, demanding a reevaluation of the injustices suffered and holding those responsible to the uncompromising standards of fairness and equity. The echoes of their demand resonate not only in the hallowed halls of recognition but in the collective conscience of a society seeking redemption from historical wrongs.\n\nKey Points:\n\n	Systemic Injustice: The Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe\'s plight is not merely a bureaucratic hurdle but a stark example of systemic injustice. The intentional imposition of federal regulations not only delayed their progress but systematically deprived them of their inherent right to state recognition.\n\n	Denial of Due Process: The imposed federal regulatory requirements, a clear violation of due process, robbed the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe of a fair and impartial evaluation. This denial strikes at the heart of constitutional principles, leaving the tribe exposed to the whims of biased entities.\n\n	Public Humiliation and Defamation: The tribe has endured public humiliation and defamation orchestrated by the Advisory Board. Their personal records were laid bare, fostering an environment where the tribe\'s history and legitimacy were scrutinized without context or understanding.\n\n	Historical Erasure: The criteria for recognition, forcing tribes to prove their existence prior to \"first contact\" under modern-day Virginia, represents a deliberate attempt to erase historical realities. This erasure perpetuates the legacy of Walter Plecker and undermines the rich history of the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe in the region.\n\n	A Call for Justice: The Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe\'s call for justice is not just a plea for recognition; it is a demand for accountability and fairness. The tribe refuses to accept their continued victimization and vows to seek justice for the injustices they have endured.\n\nIn concluding this narrative of injustice, the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe stands resilient, poised not only to fight for recognition but to seek justice for the systemic violations they have faced. The path to justice may involve unveiling the illusory nature of the Advisory Board\'s biased proceedings, challenging the legality of imposed federal regulations, and holding accountable those who have perpetuated the injustice.\n\nAs the Wolf Creek Cherokee Tribe navigates the murky waters of recognition, the underlying tone suggests an unyielding determination to explore legal avenues, seeking justice through potential liabilities. \n\n\nThe tribe\'s commitment to justice echoes through the centuries, as they refuse to succumb to the same oppressive forces that have marred their history. \n\nThe pursuit of recognition is not merely administrative; it is a quest for retribution and restoration, compelling those responsible for the injustice to face the consequences of their actions.  The factual documentation is on official public record, which will be exposed in future manuscript.','comment','published','n','2024-01-28 23:23:23','2024-01-28 23:23:23'),
(16151,90417,73272,'Rick','example@example.com',NULL,'127.58.165.237','Like sands through the hourglass, these are the days of our lives... \n\nThat last post reads like some kind of romance novel. Is this the best people can offer? \n\nRead the bill. Literally read the bill and the text from all the past bills that failed. This is nothing more than a group who is appropriating the history, archaeology and cultures of other people. They are literally claiming local Powhatan history as their own. They are stating that simply participating in events somehow legitimizes them (how is that even a thing?). \n\nThis continues to be one of the most poorly written HR\'s put forward. Read HJ14\'s content. Anyone with a rudimentary understanding of Virginia history (let along Virginia Indian history) should question the claims made as they are preposterous and unsupported by fact. It is disappointing Delegate McQuinn continues to put forward such a poorly written and unsupported HJ bill when unbiased experts and staff assembled by the Commonwealth followed the GA\'s directive from last year to review the claims made and found them lacking.\n\nThis bill should be added to the ash heap of prior efforts given the lack of real evidence to support the claims made.','comment','published','n','2024-01-29 04:12:35','2024-01-29 04:12:35'),
(16152,90419,73272,'Gary','example@example.com',NULL,'127.102.158.164','Such a uneducated statement Rick..\nTake a look at all the Tribes in Virginia and see how they received State recognition.\n\nSee how they received federal recognition.\n\nNative Americans went anywhere they wanted and were certainly free.\n\nThe current native American history is verbally passed down not very correct when told by non natives.\n\n\nYou yourself evidently spoke with fork tongue.','comment','published','n','2024-01-29 18:27:01','2024-01-29 18:27:01'),
(16229,90810,73272,'Zac','example@example.com',NULL,'127.231.176.202','The Wolf Creek should not be recognized because they cannot demonstrate Cherokee heritage or historical continuity of an Indian community. Further, all Cherokee land claims were extinguished around by or around the time of the Revolution. Even if they had Cherokee ancestry, their status as a Cherokee community had to have ended (voluntarily, I might add). Even when Cherokees had land claims in modern-day Virginia, the claims and usage were peripheral. They served as hunting grounds, trade routes, and other ancillary purposes, but did not generally serve primary functions such as long-term habitation. The Wolf Creek do not have any factual claim of being a historical Indian community with relevant ties to Virginia.','comment','published','n','2024-03-04 20:52:10','2024-03-04 20:52:10');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16011,90233,72955,'Rudie Verougstraete','example@example.com',NULL,'127.224.72.240','No one who isn\'t in the military or a member of law enforcement needs an assault rifle. You don\'t need it for hunting, you don\'t need it as part of a hobby. And you can\'t tell me about the wishes of the Founding Fathers...no one could possibly have anticipated guns that can kill hundreds within minutes in their time (and also that stubborn clause about \"well regulated militia\"). If owning a firearm is truly about self-defense or hunting, rather than projecting a certain image, then a hand gun (or rifle) is sufficient.','comment','published','n','2024-01-13 13:52:31','2024-01-13 13:52:31'),
(16190,90521,72955,'David Hunter','example@example.com',NULL,'127.76.201.159','The devil is always in the details. If you read into the bill, they are using this definition of assault weapon: \n\n\"Assault firearm\" means any semi-automatic center-fire rifle or pistol which expels single or multiple projectiles by action of an explosion of a combustible material and is equipped at the time of the offense with a magazine which will hold more than 20 rounds of ammunition or designed by the manufacturer to accommodate a silencer or equipped with a folding stock.\n\nThis will effectively outlaw a huge percentage of weapons on the market.','comment','published','n','2024-02-06 22:29:00','2024-02-06 22:29:00'),
(16191,5059,72955,'Waldo Jaquith','example@example.com','https://waldo.jaquith.org/','127.36.20.254','Boy, I hope so.\n\nI\'m a long-time gun owner. I own several. I can\'t see why in the world I\'d ever need more than 20 rounds in a magazine, why I\'d need a silencer, or why I\'d need a folding stock. But I <em>do</em> know why murderers want those.','comment','published','n','2024-02-07 01:16:30','2024-02-07 01:16:30');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16272,91026,78195,'Bill Peabody','example@example.com',NULL,'127.181.206.192','Home schooled generally  outperform public school kids. This bill is a no.','comment','published','n','2025-01-12 19:55:28','2025-01-12 19:55:28'),
(16288,91055,78195,'Suzi Croes','example@example.com','https://vicfa.org','127.117.66.157','Senator Pekarsky, assumes that religious homeschoolers need monitoring to make sure that they are up to \"her\" standards of learning  as a public servant in the educational system.  That will never do.  It is like saying that apples and oranges are the same because they are both fruit.  They are-but very different.  She should respect the dedication it takes to homeschool and the individual right to raise our children with religious convictions of our choosing.','comment','published','n','2025-01-16 05:51:30','2025-01-16 05:51:30');
